<p>In Texas, a public adjuster's total commission cannot exceed 10% of the insurance settlement (Tex. Ins. Code §4102.104). The fee is a contingency: it is taken as a percentage of each claim payment the insurer makes, so if the insurer pays nothing, the public adjuster earns nothing. There is one important exception: if your insurer pays, or commits in writing to pay, your policy limits on a total fire loss within 72 hours of the claim being reported, a percentage fee is not allowed at all.</p>
<p>If you have seen 15% or 20% quoted online, that does not apply here. In Texas, anything over 10% is not permitted.</p>

<div class="keyfacts">
<div><span>10%</span><p>maximum total commission on the settlement (§4102.104)</p></div>
<div><span>72 hours</span><p>to cancel a signed public adjuster contract (§4102.103)</p></div>
<div><span>$0</span><p>upfront with our firm; the first claim review is free</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What the 10% cap actually covers</h2>
<p>The cap applies to the public adjuster's <em>total</em> commission. A contract that quotes a percentage and then adds "administrative," "file opening" or "processing" charges on top deserves a hard look and a question about how the total stays within the cap.</p>
<p>The percentage must be written into the contract, which must be on a TDI-approved form and give you 72 hours to rescind (§4102.103). Within the cap, the rate is negotiated between you and the adjuster. What you agree to on paper is what applies, so read the fee section closely. Our guide to <a href="/post/smart-property-owners-guide-to-choosing-the-right-public-adjuster/">choosing a public adjuster</a> covers the rest of the contract.</p>

<h2>Why a percentage and not an hourly rate</h2>
<p>A contingency fee means the adjuster carries the risk. If the insurer pays nothing, you owe no percentage. It also ties the adjuster's pay to the result, so the incentive is to document every covered item rather than to bill hours. The trade-off is that on a claim that was already fairly priced, a percentage can cost you more than the work added. That is why Texas sets a ceiling, and why you should ask what the fee would be in dollars on your claim before you sign.</p>

<h2>The total fire loss exception</h2>
<div class="callout callout--law"><p><strong>Texas law:</strong> If the insurer pays, or commits in writing to pay, the policy limits on a total fire loss within 72 hours after the claim is reported, a public adjuster may not take a percentage of that payment. Only reasonable compensation based on time actually spent is allowed (§4102.104).</p></div>
<p>The logic is simple. If the house is gone and the insurer pays the full limit right away, there was nothing for an adjuster to negotiate, so there is nothing to take a percentage of. It is not the usual case, because many fire claims involve inspections, cause-and-origin questions and a contents inventory that take far longer than 72 hours. But if it happens to you, know the rule. More on fire claims in <a href="/post/fire-damage-insurance/">fire damage insurance in Texas</a>.</p>

<h2>How the percentage is applied to your payments</h2>
<p>Insurers rarely pay a claim in one check. The fee follows the money as it arrives.</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li><strong>Your deductible is never paid, so it is never part of the fee.</strong> The insurer subtracts it before paying; the percentage applies to what is actually paid.</li>
<li><strong>Actual cash value first, depreciation later.</strong> On replacement cost policies, insurers often pay actual cash value (replacement cost minus depreciation and deductible) first, then release recoverable depreciation after repairs are done. The fee applies to each payment as it is made.</li>
<li><strong>Payments made before you hired the adjuster.</strong> This is where contracts differ. Some apply the percentage only to money paid after you sign; others apply it to the whole claim. Ask, and make sure the answer is in writing before you sign.</li>
<li><strong>Every coverage on the claim.</strong> Ask whether the fee applies to dwelling, other structures, contents and additional living expenses alike. It is a fair question, and the contract should answer it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Who is named on the check</h2>
<p>Texas law requires that you, the insured, be a payee on every claim check, and a public adjuster may never sign or endorse a check on your behalf (§4102.104). You deposit the money; the fee is then paid as the contract describes.</p>
<p>Other names often appear too. If you have a mortgage, the lender is usually named on dwelling checks under the policy's mortgage clause, and you may need to follow the lender's process to have funds released for repairs. Ask your public adjuster up front exactly whose names will be on the checks and how the fee is collected, so nothing surprises you when the first payment arrives.</p>

