<h2>Worked examples</h2>
<p>These are hypothetical round numbers to show the mechanics. They are not results from real claims, and they use the 10% maximum for simplicity.</p>

<h3>Example 1: hired before the claim was filed</h3>
<p>A roof and interior claim. The agreed replacement cost is $40,000. The policy has a 1% wind and hail deductible on $300,000 of dwelling coverage, so the deductible is $3,000. Depreciation of $8,000 is withheld until repairs are complete.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Payment</th><th>Insurer pays</th><th>Fee at 10%</th><th>You receive</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Actual cash value ($40,000 − $8,000 − $3,000)</td><td>$29,000</td><td>$2,900</td><td>$26,100</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recoverable depreciation, after repairs</td><td>$8,000</td><td>$800</td><td>$7,200</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>$37,000</strong></td><td><strong>$3,700</strong></td><td><strong>$33,300</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Notice the fee is 10% of $37,000, the amount actually paid, not of the $40,000 replacement cost. The $3,000 deductible is yours either way.</p>

<h3>Example 2: hired after a first payment</h3>
<p>The insurer has already paid $10,000. After re-inspection and a supplemented estimate, total payments on the claim reach $30,000, so $20,000 more is paid after the public adjuster is hired.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>If the contract charges on</th><th>Fee at 10%</th><th>Total you keep from $30,000</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Only new money ($20,000)</td><td>$2,000</td><td>$28,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>The whole claim ($30,000)</td><td>$3,000</td><td>$27,000</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The $1,000 difference is why the "what is the fee based on" question matters. Compare either result with the $10,000 you would have had without the extra payments.</p>

<h3>Example 3: total fire loss, paid at once</h3>
<p>A home burns to the slab. Within 72 hours of the claim being reported, the insurer commits in writing to pay the $250,000 dwelling limit. No percentage fee applies to that payment; the public adjuster may charge only reasonable compensation for time actually spent.</p>

<h2>Fee terms to confirm before you sign</h2>
<p>The percentage is the headline, but these terms decide what you actually pay. Each should be answered in the contract, not in conversation.</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li><strong>The rate.</strong> A single percentage, at or below 10%.</li>
<li><strong>The base.</strong> All payments on the claim, or only payments after the contract date.</li>
<li><strong>Coverages included.</strong> Dwelling, other structures, contents, additional living expenses, business income on a commercial claim.</li>
<li><strong>Extra charges.</strong> Whether anything is billed on top of the percentage, and how the total stays within the cap.</li>
<li><strong>Appraisal costs.</strong> Who pays your appraiser and your share of the umpire if the claim goes to appraisal.</li>
<li><strong>Ending the contract.</strong> Your 72-hour rescission right, and what the contract says about cancellation after that.</li>
<li><strong>Payment mechanics.</strong> Whose names will be on each check, and when and how the fee is paid.</li>
</ul>

